::: {.doc-subject}

Check if an undirected graph is a split graph.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L38551-L38663){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.is_split_graph()
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Is split graph](../assets/graphs/is-split-graph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Left: a triangle A, B, C, shaded as the clique, with D joined to A and B and E joined to A; D and E share no edge, so they form an independent set and is_split_graph() returns True. Right: the four-cycle A, B, C, D (in the accent color) cannot be split that way, so is_split_graph() returns False." width="100%"}

A graph is *split* when its nodes can be divided into a clique (every pair joined) and
an independent set (no pair joined), with any edges at all between the two parts.
Stars, complete graphs and threshold graphs are split; the square and the five-cycle
are not. Split graphs are exactly the graphs that are chordal with a chordal
complement, and they model core-periphery structure in its purest form: a fully
connected core and a periphery whose members link only to the core.

Parallel edges count once and self-loops are ignored.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   `True` if the graph is split. The empty graph is.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If the graph is directed.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~is_complete_split_graph`) 

:   Split, with every clique node joined to every other node.

[](`~is_chordal`) 

:   Split graphs are the chordal graphs with a chordal complement.

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

Hammer and Simeone (1981) showed that being split depends only on the degree sequence.
With the degrees sorted in decreasing order $d_1 \ge \dots \ge d_n$ and $m$ the largest
$i$ with $d_i \ge i - 1$, the graph is split exactly when
$\sum_{i \le m} d_i = m(m - 1) + \sum_{i > m} d_i$, in which case the $m$ nodes of
highest degree form the clique. This takes $O(V \log V + E)$ time. Results match
NetworkX pull request #8126's `is_split_graph`, which tests that the graph and its
complement are both chordal.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

A triangle with a pendant node splits into the triangle (a clique) and the pendant node
(an independent set). A square can't be split that way:

::: {#6b9bf6f1 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

kite = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("A", "B"), ("A", "C"), ("B", "C"), ("C", "D")]
)

kite.is_split_graph(), ec.Graph().add_cycle_graph(4).is_split_graph()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(True, False)
```
:::
:::


### A core and a periphery

The three partners of a small firm all work with one another, and each of its five
clients works with one or two of them. Clients don't work together, so the firm splits
into a clique, the partners, and an independent set, the clients:

::: {#fbd82bfd .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
firm = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("Ana", "Ben"), ("Ana", "Cho"), ("Ben", "Cho"), ("Dee", "Ana"),
     ("Eli", "Ana"), ("Eli", "Ben"), ("Fay", "Ben"), ("Gus", "Cho"),
     ("Hal", "Cho"), ("Hal", "Ana")]
)
firm.set_node_attrs(
    "role", {n: "partner" if n in ("Ana", "Ben", "Cho") else "client"
             for n in firm.get_node_df()["label"]}
)
firm.is_split_graph()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
True
```
:::
:::


Once Dee and Fay start working together (right), Dee, Ana, Ben and Fay form a cycle of
four with no chord, and the graph is no longer split:

::: {layout-ncol="2"}

::: {#e3bfab31 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
firm.layout_kamada_kawai().plot(
    layout="pos", color_by="role", label_by="label",
    width=320, height=360, legend=False,
)
```

